00:45:56.000 --> 00:46:28.000
So the lemonade principle is the idea that before you throw stuff away, in fact, most of the time you can make, you know, a lot of good businesses out of trash and then it could become like a very successful product. There is a final principle Saras talks about. Pilots in the plane. Expert entrepreneurs don't merely react to their environment. They actively shape it. They make their own luck, if you will.
所以柠檬汁原则的意思是，在把东西扔掉之前，事实上，大多数时候你可以用废品做出很多好的生意，然后它就能变成一个非常成功的产物。萨拉斯提到的最后一个原则是：飞机上的飞行员。专业创业者不仅仅是对环境做出反应。他们还在积极地塑造环境。可以说，他们在创造自己的运气。

86
00:46:28.000 --> 00:47:03.000
Take a look at Netflix. In the early 2000s, the company's DVD rental-by-mail model was in danger of becoming obsolete. Netflix pivoted to become a streaming service. The company wasn't just trying to win a share of an existing market. They were creating a new market. In so doing, they changed the way we all consume entertainment. They invented their own future. As Saras talked to these entrepreneurs, she found that there was a presiding mindset over all of these principles. Saras calls this mindset, effectuation.
看看奈飞（Netflix）。在2000年代初，该公司通过邮寄租赁DVD的模式正面临被淘汰的危险。奈飞转型成为了一家流媒体服务商。该公司不仅仅是为了赢得现有市场的一份份额。他们正在创造一个新市场。在此过程中，他们改变了我们所有人消费娱乐的方式。他们发明了自己的未来。随着萨拉斯与这些创业者交谈，她发现所有这些原则背后都有一个统领全局的心态。萨拉斯将这种心态称之为：效果逻辑（Effectuation）。

87
00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:36.000
Saras calls this mindset, effectuation. So effectual thinking is a way to minimize predictive information while making decisions under great uncertainty. And the way you minimize predictive information is you're always dividing the world into things within your control and things outside your control. You ignore things outside your control and continuously use things within your control and things outside your control. You ignore things outside your control and continuously use things within your control to shape and co- create the future. That's what effectual thinking is and so you end up creating
萨拉斯将这种心态称之为：效果逻辑。因此，效果逻辑思维是一种在巨大的不确定性下做出决策的同时将预测性信息最小化的方法。而你将预测性信息最小化的方法是，你总是将世界划分为在你控制范围内的事情和不在你控制范围内的事情。你忽略那些不在你控制范围内的事情，并不断利用在你控制范围内的事情，去塑造和共同创造未来。这就是效果逻辑思维，所以你最终会在世界上创造出

88
00:47:36.000 --> 00:48:01.000
new effects in the world, even effects that you did not begin with. I mean in some ways one of the lessons here is that the really successful entrepreneurs have the humility to know that the future is not controllable. And in some ways, once you accept that, then you realize it doesn't make a ton of sense to try and figure out where you're going, try and figure out the destination, try and figure out the big picture strategy.
新的影响，甚至是那些你一开始没有预料到的影响。我的意思是，从某种意义上说，这里的教训之一是，真正成功的创业者具备一种谦逊的态度，深知未来是无法控制的。从某种程度上说，一旦你接受了这一点，你就会意识到，试图去弄清楚你要去哪里、试图找出目的地、试图找出宏观战略，其实并没有多大意义。

89
00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:38.000
All of that, in some ways, is unknowable. Absolutely. You don't have to think, all of that in some ways is unknowable. Absolutely. You don't have to think about all of these other things that people worry about. And then the beauty of it is you actually end up achieving some of those big things precisely because you are not really focusing all your time and attention on it. and attention on it. Saras Sarasvati teaches entrepreneurship at the University of Virginia's Darden School of Business. She's the author of Effectuation, Elements of Entrepreneurial Expertise.
